(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-ii-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies II Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In the Lugano staging of lymphoma, involved lymph node regions both above and below the diaphragm are which stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the Lugano classification (a modified Ann Arbor system), stage 3 is two or more lymph node regions above and below the diaphragm. Stage 2 keeps them on the same side; stage 4 is widespread disease in multiple organs. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"In the R-ISS (Revised International Staging System), which finding belongs to stage I?","choices":["High lactate dehydrogenase","Albumin of at least 3.5 g/dL","Beta-2 microglobulin of 5.5 mg/L or more","Deletion 17p"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage I is beta-2 microglobulin under 3.5 mg/L, albumin of at least 3.5 g/dL, standard-risk chromosomal abnormalities and a normal lactate dehydrogenase. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the usual management of follicular lymphoma that causes no symptoms?","choices":["Watch and wait","Immediate multi-agent chemotherapy","Emergent hematology transfer","Allogeneic stem cell transplant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Follicular lymphoma frequently needs no treatment (watch and wait), though it may be treated with intermittent low-intensity therapy if symptomatic; high-risk disease may need more aggressive therapy. It is incurable, but often needs no treatment or only intermittent treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 16, 27, 34, 42"},{"q":"How is venetoclax typically given in chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["As maintenance after transplant","With an antibody, for a fixed time","Alone, continued indefinitely","With radiation, for one node"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Venetoclax, the B-cell lymphoma 2 inhibitor, is combined with monoclonal antibodies for a fixed duration. That contrasts with Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitors, which are usually continuous when used alone. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 27, 53"},{"q":"In chronic lymphocytic leukemia, rapid progression that ends watchful waiting is a lymphocyte count doubling within what time?","choices":["Under 2 months","Under 3 months","Under 6 months","Under 12 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Observation continues until rapid progression, defined as the lymphocyte count doubling in under 6 months, or until disease-related symptoms appear. Either one is the indication to start treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 11"},{"q":"How does Hodgkin lymphoma typically spread?","choices":["Mainly to the bone","Contiguously, node to node","Randomly, skipping nodes","Mainly to the skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is marked by contiguous spread through the lymph nodes in an orderly fashion. Its malignant cells are few binucleated Reed-Sternberg cells among numerous immune effector cells. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 22, 36, 48"},{"q":"In tumor lysis syndrome, which released product carries the largest risk of death?","choices":["Uric acid","Calcium","Potassium","Phosphorus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome releases potassium, phosphorus and uric acid; the largest risk is hyperkalemia, which leads to arrhythmia and potential death. It is a medical emergency needing aggressive intravenous fluids. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which non-Hodgkin lymphoma is classed as indolent?","choices":["Burkitt lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Adult T-cell lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-Hodgkin lymphomas range from very aggressive (Burkitt, diffuse large B-cell, T-cell leukemia/lymphoma) to indolent (follicular, marginal zone, small lymphocytic). Follicular lymphoma is the most common indolent histology. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 15, 16"},{"q":"Which group of Hodgkin lymphoma patients has inferior treatment results?","choices":["Older patients","Patients in their 20s","Non-bulky stage I disease","Patients with cervical nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inferior results are seen with older patients, those with bulky disease and those with certain subtypes (lymphocyte depleted or mixed cellularity). Hodgkin lymphoma is otherwise generally curable. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 37, 38, 42"},{"q":"Which infection can cause an atypical lymphocytosis that mimics chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["Pertussis","Helicobacter pylori","Hepatitis C","Lyme disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atypical lymphocytosis can be seen with some infections, including pertussis, so an infection-related rise belongs in the differential of a high lymphocyte count before chronic lymphocytic leukemia is called. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 17, 23"},{"q":"In the Rai classification, lymphocytosis with anemia, lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly, and normal platelets, is which stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rai stage III is lymphocytosis and anemia, with or without lymphadenopathy and/or organomegaly. Anemia outranks the nodes and spleen, and with normal platelets the stage does not reach IV. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which imaging is classically used to find the bone lesions of multiple myeloma?","choices":["Chest X-ray alone","Abdominal ultrasound","Brain magnetic resonance imaging","Whole-body computed tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The work-up for myeloma includes imaging for bone lesions, classically whole-body computed tomography, along with protein electrophoresis, light chains, beta-2 microglobulin and bone marrow biopsy with flow cytometry. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which organ enlargement is most often found on exam in chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["Nasal mass","Splenomegaly","Hepatomegaly","Mediastinal mass"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. On exam, splenomegaly is found in about 40% of patients, while hepatomegaly is less common. Painless, firm, mobile lymphadenopathy (50-90% of cases) is the other common finding on exam. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 7, 21, 24, 38"},{"q":"Outside of lymphoma, in which setting can tumor lysis syndrome also be seen?","choices":["Monoclonal B lymphocytosis below 5,000","Acute leukemia with marked leukocytosis","Asymptomatic monoclonal gammopathy","Untreated indolent follicular lymphoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome can be seen outside lymphomas, in acute lymphocytic leukemia and acute myelogenous leukemia with marked leukocytosis. Within lymphoma it can occur spontaneously in highly aggressive types such as Burkitt. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 16, 28, 44"},{"q":"An M-protein of 1.5 g/dL, clonal marrow plasma cells of 4% and no anemia, bone lesions or renal failure fit which disorder?","choices":["Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia)","MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance)","Smoldering multiple myeloma, intermediate stage","Active multiple myeloma with end-organ damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An M-protein below 3 g/dL (1.5 g/dL), clonal marrow plasma cells below 10% (4%) and no myeloma-defining characteristics meet every part of MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance), which progresses at about 1% per year. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 18, 50, 56, 57"},{"q":"How does the long-term prognosis of promptly treated Burkitt lymphoma differ between children and adults?","choices":["50-90% in children, over 90% in adults","Equal, about 60% in both groups","Poor in both, under 20%","Over 90% in children, 50-90% in adults"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With early identification and multi-agent chemotherapy, long-term prognosis is excellent: above 90% in children and 50-90% in adults. Prompt treatment with emergent hematology referral is the key. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is fine needle aspiration avoided when lymphoma is suspected?","choices":["It cannot show node architecture","It spreads the lymphoma","It triggers tumor lysis","It needs general anesthesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fine needle aspiration is avoided because an excisional biopsy is required to observe the lymph node architecture, and aspiration may miss the diagnosis. Biopsy is reserved for significant (2 cm), persistent and progressively enlarging nodes. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 25, 28"},{"q":"If Hodgkin lymphoma relapses after first-line treatment, what may be required?","choices":["Single-node radiation","Observation alone","Allogeneic stem cell transplant","Autologous transplant and maintenance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma that relapses after first-line treatment may require an allogeneic stem cell transplant. Overall, more than 90% of patients are alive at 5 years. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 42, 16, 27, 53"},{"q":"What is the backbone of treatment for newly diagnosed multiple myeloma?","choices":["Novel cellular therapy as first treatment","Proteasome inhibitor, immunomodulatory drug and steroid","Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor given continuously","Rituximab with four-drug chemotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Newly diagnosed patients are treated with a 3- or 4-drug regimen built on a proteasome inhibitor and immunomodulatory drug backbone with steroids. Transplant candidates add autologous transplant and maintenance. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 53, 11, 27"},{"q":"At what rate does monoclonal B lymphocytosis progress to chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["1-2% per year","About 10% per year","About 25% per year","About 50% per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Monoclonal B lymphocytosis, a count below 5,000 with no nodes, organomegaly, cytopenias or symptoms, progresses to chronic lymphocytic leukemia at a rate of 1-2% per year. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which T-cell lymphoma often affects the nasal region?","choices":["Hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma","Intestinal T-cell lymphoma","Peripheral T-cell, unspecified type","Extranodal natural killer/T-cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Extranodal natural killer/T-cell lymphoma often affects the nasal region. It is one of the diverse T-cell lymphomas, alongside peripheral T-cell (most common), anaplastic large cell and hepatosplenic types. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which statement describes the prognosis of indolent lymphomas?","choices":["Curable in about half with chemotherapy","Curable in over 90% at 5 years","Median survival under two years","Incurable, but survival often 10-15+ years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Indolent lymphomas are incurable but survival is often 10-15 or more years with intermittent treatment, with a risk of progression to an aggressive, difficult-to-treat lymphoma. Aggressive lymphomas, by contrast, are often treatable. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 30, 42"},{"q":"Which set of findings defines MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance)?","choices":["M-protein under 3 g/dL, plasma cells under 10%","M-protein over 3 g/dL, plasma cells 10-60%","Plasma cells 10% or more with bone lesions","Lymphocytes under 5,000 with no nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance) is by definition asymptomatic: M-protein below 3 g/dL, clonal bone marrow plasma cells below 10% and no myeloma-defining characteristics. It progresses to myeloma at about 1% per year. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 47, 50, 56"},{"q":"Where is the lymphadenopathy of Hodgkin lymphoma usually found?","choices":["Cervical nodes","Inguinal nodes","Axillary nodes","Mesenteric nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is characterized by painless asymptomatic lymphadenopathy (70%), usually cervical, but it could be supraclavicular or mediastinal. A mediastinal mass may show on chest X-ray. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is tumor lysis syndrome prevented before treating an aggressive lymphoma?","choices":["Fluid restriction during therapy","Allopurinol and fluids beforehand","Potassium replacement beforehand","Delaying treatment until stable"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome should be anticipated in aggressive lymphoma, and patients are given allopurinol (a hypouricemic agent) and fluids in advance. Patients are told to drink plenty of fluids and take allopurinol on starting treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 28, 34"},{"q":"What causes the renal dysfunction of multiple myeloma?","choices":["Light chain deposition","Uric acid crystals","Hyperviscosity","Osteoclast activation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Renal dysfunction in myeloma often comes from light chain and calcium phosphate deposition. Anemia is multifactorial, from renal dysfunction and bone marrow involvement. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 45, 28"},{"q":"Which group is at higher risk of developing multiple myeloma?","choices":["People of Jewish descent","People of Caribbean descent","People from New Guinea","African Americans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. African Americans are at higher risk of developing multiple myeloma than other races. Other risks are older age (most diagnoses over 65), family history, prior monoclonal gammopathy or smoldering myeloma, and radiation, chemical or occupational exposures. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 46, 6, 21, 32"},{"q":"What is the median age at diagnosis of chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["40 years","55 years","70 years","85 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The median age at diagnosis, and at onset, of chronic lymphocytic leukemia is 70 years, so it is a disease of older adults. That elderly onset is one reason many patients are expected to die with the disease rather than from it. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 4, 6"},{"q":"About what share of non-Hodgkin lymphoma patients develop extranodal disease?","choices":["About 10%","About 25%","About 50%","About 90%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Approximately half of patients develop extranodal disease, lymphoma outside the lymph nodes, such as central nervous system lymphoma. Peripheral lymphadenopathy is present in more than two thirds. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which agent used in chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor?","choices":["Venetoclax","Obinutuzumab","Allopurinol","Ibrutinib"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitors are ibrutinib, acalabrutinib and zanubrutinib, usually given continuously when used as monotherapy. They are one of the targeted therapies for chronic lymphocytic leukemia. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 28"}]};
